## Why This Is Asked
Tests runtime extensibility and correctness of flag evaluation, including versioning and thread-safety.

## Key Concepts
- Strategy pattern for evaluation logic
- Registry/Factory for runtime additions
- Thread-safety and version-keying
- Performance implications of dynamic dispatch

## Code Example
```typescript
interface User { id: string; segments: string[]; }
interface Flag { name: string; version: string; defaultValue?: boolean; }

interface FlagEvaluationStrategy {
  evaluate(user: User, flag: Flag): boolean;
}
type StrategyFactory = () => FlagEvaluationStrategy;

class FlagRegistry {
  private map = new Map<string, StrategyFactory>();
  register(key: string, factory: StrategyFactory) { this.map.set(key, factory); }
  get(key: string): FlagEvaluationStrategy {
    const f = this.map.get(key);
    if (!f) throw new Error(`No strategy registered for ${key}`);
    return f();
  }
}
class FlagEvaluator {
  constructor(private registry: FlagRegistry) {}
  eval(flag: Flag, user: User): boolean {
    const strat = this.registry.get(`${flag.name}|${flag.version}`);
    return strat.evaluate(user, flag);
  }
}
class PercentStrategy implements FlagEvaluationStrategy {
  constructor(private percent: number) {}
  evaluate(user: User, flag: Flag): boolean {
    const r = (parseInt(user.id.slice(-4), 10) % 100) < this.percent;
    return r;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes the ability to design runtime-extensible behavior with safe hot-swapping—a core concern in data pipelines at scale. It tests the candidate's grasp of the Strategy and Registry patterns, concurrency control, and observability.

## Key Concepts

- **Strategy Pattern**: Policy interface defines behavior that can be swapped dynamically
- **Registry Pattern**: Maps (source, datatype) tuples to policy factories for runtime lookup
- **Hot-Swapping**: Use immutable snapshots or atomic reference swaps to avoid locking during policy evaluation
- **Observability**: Metrics for policy decisions, latency histograms, and distributed tracing context

## Code Example

```typescript
interface Policy {
  apply(event: Event, ctx: Context): Action;
}

type Action = 
  | { kind: 'forward' }
  | { kind: 'delay'; value: number }  // delay in ms
  | { kind: 'drop' };

type PolicyFactory = () => Policy;

class PolicyRegistry {
  private policies = new Map<string, PolicyFactory>();
  private version = 0;

  register(source: string, datatype: string, factory: PolicyFactory): void {
    this.policies.set(`${source}::${datatype}`, factory);
    this.version++;
  }

  get(source: string, datatype: string): Policy | undefined {
    return this.policies.get(`${source}::${datatype}`)?.();
  }
}

class PolicyEngine {
  private registry: PolicyRegistry;
  private snapshot: Map<string, Policy>;
  private lock = new RWLock();

  constructor(registry: PolicyRegistry) {
    this.registry = registry;
    this.snapshot = this.buildSnapshot();
  }

  private buildSnapshot(): Map<string, Policy> {
    const snap = new Map<string, Policy>();
    // Build immutable snapshot for lock-free reads
    return snap;
  }

  reload(): void {
    this.lock.write(() => {
      this.snapshot = this.buildSnapshot();
    });
  }

  evaluate(event: Event, ctx: Context): Action {
    return this.lock.read(() => {
      const policy = this.snapshot.get(`${event.source}::${event.datatype}`);
      return policy?.apply(event, ctx) ?? { kind: 'forward' };
    });
  }
}
```

## Thread-Safety Strategy

- **Read-Mostly Pattern**: Use a read-write lock so policy evaluation (read) is lock-free while updates acquire a write lock
- **Immutable Snapshots**: Replace the entire policy map atomically via `AtomicReference` so readers never see partial states
- **Copy-on-Write**: For lower throughput, use `CopyOnWriteArrayMap` for thread-safe iteration

## Observability

- **Metrics**: Emit counters for `policy_applied`, `events_delayed`, `events_dropped`, `circuit_breaker_open`
- **Tracing**: Pass trace context through `ctx`; annotate spans with policy decisions
- **Logging**: Debug-level logs for policy selection and action taken

## Usage Example

```typescript
const registry = new PolicyRegistry();

registry.register('sensor-a', 'temperature', () => new ThrottlePolicy(100));
registry.register('sensor-a', 'alert', () => new CircuitBreakerPolicy(5, 30000));
registry.register('api-gateway', 'request', () => new BackpressurePolicy(1000));

const engine = new PolicyEngine(registry);

// Runtime hot-swap: replace policy without restart
registry.register('sensor-a', 'temperature', () => new ThrottlePolicy(50));
engine.reload();
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design runtime-extensible components using the Strategy pattern and a lightweight registry. Encourages thinking about a clean surface area, testability, and how policies interact with the core cache.

## Key Concepts

- EvictionPolicy interface: evictKey(store, capacity) and accessed(key) hooks
- EvictionRegistry: maps names to policy factories
- Cache core: get/put, fixed capacity, invokes policy to evict when needed
- Trade-offs: O(1) lookups, eviction latency, policy state maintenance

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal eviction policy interface and implementations
class EvictionPolicy {
  accessed(key) { /* optional */ }
  evictKey(store, capacity) { throw new Error('not implemented') }
}

class LRUPolicy extends EvictionPolicy {
  constructor() { this.order = new Map(); }
  accessed(key) { if (this.order.has(key)) this.order.delete(key); this.order.set(key, true); }
  evictKey(store, capacity) {
    if (store.size <= capacity) return null;
    for (let k of this.order.keys()) {
      if (store.has(k)) return k;
    }
    return store.keys().next().value || null;
  }
}

class FIFOPolicy extends EvictionPolicy {
  constructor() { this.order = new Map(); }
  accessed(key) { /* FIFO does not update on access */ }
  evictKey(store, capacity) {
    if (store.size <= capacity) return null;
    for (let k of this.order.keys()) {
      if (store.has(k)) return k;
    }
    return store.keys().next().value || null;
  }
  registerInsert(key) { if (!this.order.has(key)) this.order.set(key, true); }
}

class EvictionRegistry {
  constructor() { this.factories = new Map(); }
  register(name, factory) { this.factories.set(name, factory); }
  create(name) { const f = this.factories.get(name); return f ? f() : null; }
}

class Cache {
  constructor(capacity, registry, policyName='lru') {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.store = new Map();
    this.registry = registry;
    this.policy = registry.create(policyName) || new LRUPolicy();
  }
  setPolicy(name) { const p = this.registry.create(name); if (p) this.policy = p; }
  put(key, value) {
    this.store.set(key, value);
    if (this.policy && typeof this.policy.registerInsert === 'function') {
      this.policy.registerInsert(key);
    }
    while (this.store.size > this.capacity) {
      const k = this.policy.evictKey(this.store, this.capacity);
      if (k == null) break;
      this.store.delete(k);
    }
  }
  get(key) {
    if (!this.store.has(key)) return undefined;
    const v = this.store.get(key);
    if (typeof this.policy.accessed === 'function') this.policy.accessed(key);
    return v;
  }
}
```

